"KSI Kitchen & Bath Blog (https://blog.ksikitchens.com/all)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ## Designing a Lakefront Cottage Kitchen and Bar Built for Making Memories ... ## From Showroom Inspiration to Timeless Comfort: A Warm & Welcoming Kitchen Makeover\n\nAppliances, kitchen remodel, The Entertainer\n\n## From Dark and Dated to Warm and Welcoming: A 1970's Kitchen Transformation\n\nA Kitchen From 1970, Finally Feeling Like Home When this family first sat down with me, they'd...\n\n3 minute read\n\nRead More\n\nkitchen remodel, The Entertainer, Lakehouse, cottage\n\n## Designing a Lakefront Cottage Kitchen and Bar Built for Making Memories\n\nI love a project where a client trusts me enough to come back for round two. When this homeowner...\n\n3 minute read\n\nRead More\n\n## Downtown Living Reimagined: A Modern Condo Renovation with Warmth and Purpose\n\nWith panoramic views of the Grand River, the Calder Plaza, the Ford Museum, and the city's exciting...\n\n2 minute read\n\nRead More\n\nLaundry Room, The Entertainer, dark & moody, Basement Kitchen, Basement Bar\n\n## Inspired by Downtown Plymouth: A Speakeasy Basement Designed for Entertaining\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTaking Toast to the Next Level With Revolution Cooking - Thou Swell (https://thouswell.com/smart-toaster-toast-recipes-revolution-cooking/)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # TAKING TOAST TO THE NEXT LEVEL WITH REVOLUTION COOKING\n\nThou Swell\n\nEntertaining · December 6, 2019\n\n# TAKING TOAST TO THE NEXT LEVEL WITH REVOLUTION COOKING\n\n[Input]\n\nThis post was sponsored by Revolution Cooking(TM) as part of an Influencer Activation for Influence Central and all opinions expressed are my own.\n\nToast might be the most versatile food, and I’m thrilled to share three different takes on delicious toasts with the Revolution Cooking R180 High-Speed Smart Toaster. With precise control that takes into account the type of bread, starting temperature, and the desired doneness, the beautiful touchscreen on this toaster gives you the ability to perfect your final product! I loved exploring all of the options from waffles to English muffins, but I decided to take the average toast and share three recipes that will take you from morning to night with inspired flavor combinations. This new toaster is faster and uses less energy, smarter with custom browning and crispness, and locks in more moisture for a tastier toast. It’s the perfect gift for the foodie on your Christmas list – and you can now save 20% off the R180 Toaster with code REVCOOK20 until the end of December! Read on to see my three spins on delicious toast for any time of day.\n\n### for breakfast\n\nALMOND BUTTER, BANANA, AND LAVENDER HONEY TOAST\nThis toast is full of protein and filling flavor, with a hint of lavender to bring floral sophistication to the palette. You can either find lavender-infused honey or simply sprinkle a few lavender buds on top for the extra flavor, or substitute it completely with a dash of cinnamon instead.\n\n### for lunch\n\nLABNE, TOMATO, AND BASIL EVOO TOAST\nA savory take on toast, this one features a strained Greek yogurt as the base, but you can use any thick plain yogurt or even sour cream in its place. With a classic combination of tomatoes and basil, this one finishes off perfectly with a fresh grind of sea salt and pepper.\n\n### for after dinner\n\nDARK CHOCOLATE, SMASHED RASPBERRY, AND MARCONA ALMOND TOAST\nI made the dark chocolate base for this toast by melting a 70% cocoa bar with two tablespoons of butter, turning it into a delicious spread. Raspberries will always be my favorite fruit pairing with chocolate, and I love how the crushed Marcona almonds add the perfect hint of salt to the finish.\n\nI had so much fun putting these flavorful toast ideas together, and just as much fun using my new toaster to brown each slice of bread perfectly. Make sure to check out the R180 High-Speed Smart Toaster on the Revolution Cooking website, and sign up for their mailing list to keep up to date with their latest innovations in the kitchen. And don’t forget to take advantage of the 20% off discount code REVCOOK20 until the end of December to take your toast up a notch next year!\n\nThou Swell is a lifestyle and interior design blog named for an old jazz tune, sharing home, garden, and entertaining projects and inspiration. Stop by to find an avant-garde mix of contemporary and traditional design curated with an eye for classic Southern style.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow To DIY Limewash a Brick Home Like the Pros Do (https://notinggrace.com/how-to-limewash-a-brick-home/)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; ## How to Limewash a Brick Home ... Image: romabio masonry brush painting Classico limewash in nube gray on brick ... Now one awesome thing about Romabio’s Classico Limewash is that you are to wet the brick, so you can start limewashing once the rain passes.\n## How to Limewash a Brick Home\n\n(Some affiliate links are provided below. Full disclosure here.)\n\nProducts Needed:\n\n  * Classico Limewash – we used Nube Gray\n  * Masonry Paint Brush\n  * Ladder\n  * Hose\n  * Pressure Washer if your brick is dirty\n  * Drill\n  * Painters Paddle\n  * Goggles and Gloves\n\n\nMeet the hero of the story!\n\nRomabio Classico Limewash\n\nIt comes from dolomite lime from northern Italy and is an authentic slaked-lime paint.\n\nAnd don’t let the labeled color fool you – we chose Nube Gray – a gorgeous putty color! The product comes untinted in 5-gallon buckets in the base color of Bianco White. You can get it tinted to many different colors that work with a white base.\n\nRight out of the box, the texture appears very wet-clay like because it is shipped undiluted as lime putty. You water it down to your application preference.\n\n### Diluting the Limewash\n\nRomabio Classico Limewash is intended to be diluted and dilution rates can vary.\n\nFor our home, we mixed 2 parts Nube Gray to 1 part clean water. Basically a 50 percent water to Limewash ratio.\n\nThen you mix, mix, mix it! A painters paddle that attaches to a drill makes the mixing process easier.\n\nTIP: Now before you begin applying – make sure to wear proper safety gear, like goggles and gloves.\nWhile it’s a natural product from the earth, like any type of paint, you don’t want to get that in your eyes, so put on that eye protection! And if you have sensitive hands, the gloves are a great layer of protection.\n\nHere is how a batch of limewash looks diluted and thoroughly mixed.\n\nImage: Classico Limewash diluted in nube gray\n\nYou mix until it’s the ideal consistency of whole milk. Not too thick nor too watery.\n\nIf you add too much water, no problem – just add some more limewash until it’s the consistency you like. I like to start with less water and add more as I go.\n\nThere’s really not one correct ratio for Classico Limewash. ### Prepping Your Brick\n\nIf your brick exterior is really dirty, you will need to power wash the surface before the initial application.\n\nWhile Classico Limewash can inhibit mold growth, that doesn’t mean it covers and kills it.\n\nSo take the extra step to kill and remove any algae and moss growing on your exterior surfaces.\n\nBut if your brick is clean, you can simply use a garden hose to wet the surface of the brick.\n\nTIP: You shouldn’t apply Romabio Classico Limewash to a dry surface.\nIf you are working in direct sunlight, or have a highly porous brick, you may need to wet the brick often throughout the application process. So keep that hose handy!\n\n\nFor my limewashing technique, I channeled my inner Mister Miagi –\n\nUUUUPPP!!  DOOOWWN!\n\nImage: romabio masonry brush painting Classico limewash in nube gray on brick\n\nAll kidding aside, you do need to vary your brush strokes to ensure proper coverage.\n\nAnd don’t be scared if it looks a bit translucent while wet – Romabio tells us that’s how it’s supposed to look at first.\n\nJust apply a thin layer. It will have a different look while it’s wet, but will dry to a lighter, solid finish.\n\nHere’s a pic of our brick half wet/ half dry.\n\nImage: Classico Limewash in nube gray drying on brick\n\nThe lighter color is the dried section.\n\nJust look at how that chalky finish appears once it’s dry!  Gorgeous!!\n\nTIP: If you plan to distress the brick to get that old world feel, then this is the sweet spot.\nWatch for the brick to start turning to a solid finish.\n\nAnd the whole process is very quick. This side of our house took just only one day to complete.\n\nImage: during process limewashing my home\n\nBut one time hurdle we had to battle was the weather. It took us a couple weeks to finish the whole house.\n\nI think what we learned most in how to limewash a brick home was to be patient with nature!\n\nTo begin with we had an unusual rainy start to our summer. Now one awesome thing about Romabio’s Classico Limewash is that you are to wet the brick, so you can start limewashing once the rain passes. And you can limewash up to 3 hours before rain is predicted to start.\n\nIt’s an easy process that will transform the exterior of a home with beautiful results.\n\nWe opted to keep the brick an opaque, solid finish so we could watch the Classico Limewash patina and change slowly over the years.\n\nBut, you have the option of creating a forced weathered limewash look by rinsing it off in places with water creating a beautiful, instant patina.\n\nThat’s what we chose when we lime washed our second home. (Yes – just 6 months later. You can read that story here. Scroll down to see those pics.)\n\nTip: If you are wanting a solid finish, Romabio has created another product, called Masonry Flat that will not chip or flake and has a 20 year warranty with 2 coats.\n\nYou can apply two coats of limewash which will slow down that aging process, but if you are going to do 2 coats anyway, I would choose Romabio Masonry Flat.\n\nNow how about a bunch of before and afters to see the amazing difference!\n\nI am wowed at the finished product.\nWe have found an easy solution and are sharing why we're limewashing our brick home】\n\nI just love the flat finish on the brick.\n\nThe black pops against that gorgeous chalky lime wash.\n\nImage: black door against Nube Gray Classico Limewash\n\nImage: black door against Classico limewash nube gray\n\nImage: Limewash brick before\n\nImage: Limewash Brick after\n\nSo in one short summer, we transformed the exterior of our home from ugly brick to a fresh modern look with a simple application of limewash.\n\nIt’s low maintenance and has the added benefits of being good for the brick!\n\nImage: Limewashing a Home before\n\nImage: Romabio Classico Limewash in Nube Gray\n\nSo, if you want to check out Romabio’s product – they are now available in select Home Depot Stores, and also online!\n\nI hope this inspires you to tackle your exterior.\n\nIt’s an easy product to work with while giving stunning results!\n\nI hope this answered any questions you had about how to limewash a brick home. If you have any additional questions, I am so happy to help. Ask me in the comment section below.\n\nI will do my best to answer them as soon as I can. Thanks for always supporting our blog. We love helping you create a home you love!\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow To DIY Limewash a Brick Home Like the Pros Do (https://notinggrace.com/how-to-limewash-a-brick-home/comment-page-3/)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; # How To DIY Limewash a Brick Home Like the Pros Do ... Here’s how to Limewash a Brick home.\n\n# How To DIY Limewash a Brick Home Like the Pros Do\n\nByJennifer Fancher June 30, 2021September 6, 2023 Updated September 6, 2023\n\n45 Comments\n\nWe changed the exterior of our home this summer with stunning results! Here’s how to Limewash a Brick home. A special thank you to Romabio for collaborating with us on this post!\n\nDid you know that I loved everything about our Tennessee home except the exterior?\n\nI would drive up to this house and just wanting to keep on going past – because I didn’t want to turn into this ugly exterior.\n\nUPDATED: Since we have now limewashed 2 homes, I updated this original post. You can read more about our current lime washed home here.\n\nImage: Limewashing a Home before\n\nWouldn’t you want to drive on, too?\n\nEarlier this summer, I shared with you our plans to finally give our brick home a facelift.\n\nI loved the look of a german smear, but quickly found out it was not a diy project.\n\nThen I looked into using latex paint.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to Limewash a Brick House: A DIY Guide (https://www.allbrightpainting.com/blog/2025/september/limewash-your-brick-house-a-step-by-step-guide/)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; If you’re looking for a way to update your brick home while preserving its natural texture and charm, limewash might be the perfect solution. ... While the color palette is more limited to earthy tones, the finish is far more forgiving and requires less upkeep, as detailed in this guide to limewashing brick. ... Many pros and DIYers have great success with products like Romabio Classico Limewash.\n\n# gallery\n\nSeptember 16, 2025\n\nThere’s a certain romance to the soft, chalky finish of an old European home. It’s a look that feels both historic and fresh, adding a layer of warmth and character that modern paints can’t replicate. This beautiful, lived-in aesthetic is achieved with limewash, a classic finish made from natural materials. Unlike a solid coat of paint that hides the brick’s texture, limewash enhances it, creating subtle variations in color and a unique patina. It’s the perfect way to add significant curb appeal without chasing a fleeting trend. If you’re ready to give your home a timeless transformation, you’ve come to the right place. Let’s explore how to achieve the perfect limewash brick house.\n\nREQUEST YOUR FREE ESTIMATE\n\n### \n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n  * Think of Limewash as a Stain, Not a Paint: It bonds with your brick instead of just sitting on top. This creates a breathable surface that won't trap moisture, peel, or chip, leading to a more durable and authentic finish.\n  * Preparation and Patience Are Your Most Important Tools: The key to a consistent, professional-looking result is starting with a clean surface and testing your technique first. Apply thin coats and use water to manage drying on warm days, giving you complete control over the final texture.\n  * Embrace the Low-Maintenance, High-Character Finish: Limewash ages gracefully by developing a natural patina instead of failing like paint. Upkeep is simple, and reapplying a fresh coat years later is a straightforward process without the need for scraping or sanding.\n\n## What is Limewash? (And Why It’s Perfect for Brick)\n\nIf you’re looking for a way to update your brick home while preserving its natural texture and charm, limewash might be the perfect solution. This classic finish offers a soft, timeless look that feels both fresh and historic. Unlike a solid coat of paint, limewash works with your brick, creating a beautiful, breathable surface that ages gracefully. It’s a fantastic way to refresh your home’s exterior, add curb appeal, and protect your masonry for years to come.\n\n### The Simple Ingredients of Limewash\n\nAt its core, limewash is beautifully simple. It’s a traditional coating made from just two natural ingredients: crushed limestone that has been burned and mixed with water. That’s it. Because of its simple composition, limewash behaves very differently from modern paints. Instead of forming a film that sits on top of the brick, it soaks into the porous surface. This creates a chemical bond with the brick itself, becoming a part of the masonry rather than just a layer on top. This unique quality is what gives limewash its signature durability and character.\n\n### Why Limewash is Great for Brick\n\nThe real magic of limewash is how it protects and enhances brick. Because it penetrates the surface, it creates a breathable layer that allows moisture to escape, which is crucial for preventing water damage and maintaining the integrity of your brick. This process gives your home a unique, matte finish with a chalky texture that adds warmth and a timeless, antique look. It’s a finish that doesn’t just cover your brick—it celebrates it. ## Your Step-by-Step Guide to Applying Limewash\n\nReady to transform your brick? Applying limewash is more of an art than a science, but following a clear process is the key to getting a look you’ll love for years. Think of it as a creative project where you’re in full control of the final finish. While it’s a project many homeowners can tackle, remember that a professional touch ensures a flawless, long-lasting result. If you want peace of mind, our residential painting experts are always here to help. But if you're ready to get your hands dirty, here’s how to approach it, one step at a time.\n\n### Mix Your Limewash to the Right Consistency\n\nGetting the right consistency is your first and most important step. Think pancake batter, not watery milk. A great starting point is to combine your limewash concentrate with water at a 1:1 ratio. This 50/50 mix is usually perfect for achieving that classic, slightly transparent look. You can always add a bit more water for a thinner, more translucent effect or a little less for more opaque coverage. Before you start, stir thoroughly until the mixture is completely smooth. You don’t want any lumps, as they can affect the final texture on the wall.\n\n### Apply the First Coat\n\nBefore you dip your brush, give your brick wall a quick rinse. Using a garden hose or a pump sprayer, lightly dampen the surface until it’s wet but not dripping. This simple prep step is crucial—it prevents the porous brick from instantly sucking the water out of your limewash, giving you more time to work the finish. Once the brick is damp, load up a good masonry brush and apply a thin, even first coat. Focus on working the mixture into the mortar lines and covering the entire surface smoothly for a consistent base.\n\n### Build Your Desired Coverage\n\nThe beauty of limewash is its buildable nature. Instead of trying to get full coverage with one thick, heavy coat, the goal is to apply multiple thin layers. This method gives you far more control over the final opacity and texture. Let each coat dry to the touch before you decide if you need another one. You might find that one coat gives you the perfect weathered patina you were hoping for. Or, you may want to apply a second or even a third layer for a more solid, uniform color. Patience is your best friend here; rushing the process is the quickest way to an uneven finish.\n\n### Achieve Different Looks and Finishes\n\nThis is where you can really get creative. If you love the rustic, old-world charm of a distressed finish, you can easily create it. After applying a coat of limewash, wait anywhere from 10 to 60 minutes, then use a damp cloth or a spray bottle and a rag to gently wipe away some of the product. This technique exposes parts of the original brick, creating a beautiful, textured appearance. Feel free to experiment on a small test area to see how much pressure to apply and how long to wait to get the exact distressed look you want.\n\n### What to Do While It Dries\n\nLimewash needs time to cure properly, and the weather plays a big role. The ideal time to apply it is on a cool, overcast day. Direct sun and high heat can cause the limewash to dry too quickly, which can lead to a chalky finish or inconsistent coloring. If you’re working in the California sun, try to work in sections that are in the shade. You can also lightly mist the wall with water as it dries to slow down the process. Working in smaller, manageable sections helps you maintain a wet edge and ensures a more consistent, professional-looking result.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLakefront Cottage Kitchen Makeover (https://uk.pinterest.com/pin/lakefront-cottage-kitchen-makeover--396246467189809709/)\nciteturn3search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; Lakefront Cottage Kitchen Makeover\nLakefront Cottage Kitchen Makeover--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow To LimeWash Brick Houses | Painted Brick Tutorial | Classically Jill (https://classicallyjill.com/how-to-limewash-your-brick-house-painted-brick-tutorial/)\nciteturn3search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; By gathering these tools before you begin, you’ll be well-equipped to successfully limewash your brick home and achieve that beautiful, breathable finish that enhances the natural aesthetic of the exterior. ... If you don’t mind painting the walls inside your house, you would probably do just fine with limewashing your brick yourselves.\n\n# How To LimeWash Brick Houses | Painted Brick Tutorial\n\nCurious about how to limewash a brick house? I’ve been where you are. Researching and reading any blog post about how to limewash brick houses that I could find. Saving pins. Messaging bloggers who have done it before. I even explored how to use traditional paint on your brick vs. lime wash, and time and time again, I kept seeing houses who used Romabio limewash.\n\nThe truth is, this isn’t my first time “painting” brick. At our last fixer upper, I used Romabio limewash and limewashed the entire house myself (while Mike watched the kids). I’m a better painter than he is – we know our strengths – and had done the research on how to apply it, so I felt confident in doing it myself.\n\nThe reason I use- and continue to use – Romabio is because it’s a natural mixture that still allows your brick to breathe, unlike paint.\n\n#### Ready for a before and after of our first house?\n\nHere’s the house when we bought it.\n\nImage\n\n## Morning Of: Preparing Your Limewash\n\nThe big day is here! I recommend getting a good night’s rest beforehand and starting bright and early before the sun gets too hot. For us, that mean 60 degree temperatures in September, starting around 9:00 a.m. after we dropped our kids at school.\n\nYou’ll first need to mix your lime wash solution. Because we wanted a slightly thicker, more opaque look, we definitely were careful not to add too much water when diluting.\n\nRomabio’s formula was 2-3 gallons of water for every 4 gallons of lime wash.\n\nTo get the more opaque look, we poured 2 gallons of lime wash (about half of the tub it comes in) into our giant 5 gallon Home Depot buckets. And then we added a gallon of water to it.\n\nWe then attached this paint mixer to our drill and mixed up the lime wash this way. I’d highly recommend this tool! It does a great job of mixing the lime wash thoroughly. The mixture can be pretty dense, so you really need some power to incorporate the water into it. Imagine mixing water into mud. It’s kind of like that.\n\n## How Much Limewash Should You Buy\n\nThis is going to depend on your square footage. Romabio’s website says that one gallon will cover one coat of 450 sq ft. We used two coats.\n\nWe purchased three large 4 gallon tubs of lime wash to do our 2300 sq. foot house. We only ended up using two of those 4 gallons, but that’s mostly because we have siding on the back of our house.\n\n## How To Apply Limewash to Brick\n\nReady to paint? Your brick needs to be wet before you paint the lime wash onto it. You’ll need to do this bit by bit as you go. So you’ll spray an area with your hose, then paint the lime wash onto it. Then you’ll spray a new section beside it and paint there.\n\nBecause you need the water, I recommend having a long hose to work with. We had some areas where our hose wouldn’t reach. Also, if you only have one water spout on the outside of your house, you could get a hose splitter to have two hoses, but what we found was that this water spray bottle did the job just as well. So Mike usually took the hose and I used the spray bottle. Each one works well! And it helps to have a second water option so that two people can paint at once.\n\nWork From Top Down\n\nYou’ll want to work from top to bottom when applying your limewash. Because we have a cape cod style home, we could work from our roofline to the ground, section by section.\n\nThe reason here is that you’ll need to wet the brick, right? --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Ultimate Guide to a Limewash Brick House (https://www.allbrightpainting.com/blog/2025/september/limewashing-a-brick-house-a-complete-guide/)\nciteturn3search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last week; This guide cuts through the confusion, explaining exactly what limewash is, how it works, and why it’s the superior choice for a lasting transformation. ... If you’ve ever admired a brick home with a soft, matte, slightly rustic finish, you were likely looking at limewash.\n\nSeptember 24, 2025 | By ALLBRiGHT PAINTING\n\nThe terms \"limewash,\" \"whitewash,\" and \"German schmear\" are often used interchangeably, but they are fundamentally different techniques with very different results. Understanding the distinction is the first step toward achieving the authentic, timeless look you want for your home. True limewash is a unique, slaked-lime coating that chemically bonds with masonry for a durable, chalky finish. Whitewash is often just watered-down paint. This guide cuts through the confusion, explaining exactly what limewash is, how it works, and why it’s the superior choice for a lasting transformation. We’ll give you the clear, straightforward information you need to plan your limewash brick house project with confidence.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n  * Limewash Works With Your Brick, Not On Top of It: Unlike paint that forms a surface layer, limewash soaks into porous materials. This creates a breathable finish that won’t chip or peel, but instead ages into a beautiful, natural patina that lasts for decades.\n  * Preparation is the Key to a Flawless Finish: For a professional-looking result, focus on two critical steps before you begin: thoroughly cleaning the brick and lightly dampening the surface with water. This ensures the limewash absorbs evenly and bonds correctly for a durable application.\n  * Confirm Compatibility Before You Commit: Limewash is only suitable for unpainted and unsealed brick. Always test a small, hidden area first to see the final color, and consider hiring a professional for large homes to guarantee a safe and beautiful result.\n\n## What is Limewash?\n\nIf you’ve ever admired a brick home with a soft, matte, slightly rustic finish, you were likely looking at limewash. Unlike traditional paint that forms a solid film on top of a surface, limewash is a unique coating made from crushed limestone that has been burned and mixed with water. This simple, natural mixture penetrates porous surfaces like brick, stone, and stucco, bonding with them chemically. The result is a breathable, durable finish that won’t peel or chip the way paint can.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to Use Limewash Brick in Both Interior and Exterior Design (https://www.realthinbrick.com/post/how-to-use-limewash-brick)\nciteturn3search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; Transforming spaces like a brick fireplace in your living room or the exterior of a brick house with a classic lime wash brings warmth and sophistication to any home. ... This guide covers every detail about limewash.\nStir thoroughly to ensure the lime and pigments are evenly distributed.\n\n### Step 3: Gather Your Tools\n\n  * A high-quality masonry brush for large surfaces or detail work.\n\n  * A spray bottle with water to dampen the brick as you work.\n\n  * Drop cloths or painter’s tape to protect surrounding areas.\n\n  * A bucket or tray for mixing and holding the limewash.\n\n### Step 4: Dampen the Brick\n\nLightly dampen the brick with a spray bottle or sponge before applying limewash. The brick should be moist but not dripping.\n\n### Step 5: Apply the Limewash\n\nImage: Applying the limewash\n\nApplying the limewash|YouTube Screenshot/Your Home, Renewed\n\nDip the brush into the limewash and begin applying it in even strokes. Start at the top and work your way down to avoid drips. The first coat will look patchy, but that is normal. Limewash changes as it dries, so do not worry about achieving perfection in the initial coat. Allow this coat to dry for at least 24 hours.\n\n### Step 6: Add Additional Layers if Needed\n\nFor a more opaque finish, apply one or two more coats, letting each layer dry completely before adding the next. If you prefer a distressed look, remove some limewash with a damp cloth or sponge before it fully dries. This creates a weathered effect that reveals parts of the original brick.\n\n### Step 7: Clean Up and Let It Settle\n\nImage: Clean up the surface\n\nClean up the surface|YouTube Screenshot/Your Home, Renewed\n\nOnce you are satisfied with the look, remove any tape or drop cloths and clean your tools. It often appears darker when wet and lightens as it cures. Give it a few days to fully set and reveal its true finish.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow We DIY Limewashed Our Brick House (Step-by-Step Guide) – Hills Uncharted (https://hillsuncharted.com/how-we-diy-limewashed-our-brick-house/)\nciteturn3search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; We experimented with both a masonry brush and a paint sprayer to find what worked best for our home. ... Test a small patch of limewash on your brick to see how it dries and whether you like a distressed or solid finish. ... Did you have any unexpected challenges while DIY limewashing your brick house?\n## Step 3: Applying Romabio Classico Limewash\n\nWe experimented with both a masonry brush and a paint sprayer to find what worked best for our home.\n\n### Using a Brush:\n\n  1. We started by wetting the brick with the water hose. Use a heavy mist setting so you don’t soak the brick. If water is running down the bricks, wait and let some of that excess water soak in/evaporate before applying the limewash.\n  2. Next, we dipped a large masonry brush into the limewash mixture.\n  3. Then we slowly applied the limewash with long, sweeping strokes from left to right on the brick, working top to bottom.\n  4. We worked in small sections (about 10 sq. ft. at a time) as we worked our way around the house.\n\n### Using a Sprayer:\n\n  1. We filled a paint sprayer with the limewash mixture.\n  2. Then we sprayed evenly left to right, from top to bottom in a larger section 15sq. ft at a time.\n  3. Then we used a masonry brush to back-brush for an even finish. You can also use the masonry brush to play with the opaqueness of the limewash on the brick.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to limewash a brick home – Artofit (https://www.artofit.org/image-gallery/273945589824460422/how-to-limewash-a-brick-home/)\nciteturn3search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; ### Image gallery for: How to limewash a brick home\n\n### Image gallery for: How to limewash a brick home\n\n  * ## How to Limewash a Brick Home\n\nWe changed the exterior of our home this summer and are amazed with the stunning results! Here is how to Limewash a Brick home featuring Romabio Paints in Nube Gray.\n----------------